XT00_EVXL8_LM2_SCX04_3.raw\n20210512_EX3_UM7_Armon001_SA_EXT00_EVXL8_LM2_SCX05_1.raw\n20210512_EX3_UM7_Armon001_SA_EXT00_EVXL8_LM2_SCX05_2.raw\n20210512_EX3_UM7_Armon001_SA_EXT00_EVXL8_LM2_SCX05_3.raw\n20210512_EX3_UM7_Armon001_SA_EXT00_EVXL8_LM2_SCX05_4.raw\n20210512_EX3_UM7_Armon001_SA_EXT00_EVXL8_LM2_SCX06_1.raw\n20210512_EX3_UM7_Armon001_SA_EXT00_EVXL8_LM2_SCX06_2.raw\n20210512_EX3_UM7_Armon001_SA_EXT00_EVXL8_LM2_SCX06_3.raw\n20210521_EX3_UM7_Armon001_SA_EXT00_EVXL8_LM2_SCX04_4.raw\n20210521_EX3_UM7_Armon001_SA_EXT00_EVXL8_LM2_SCX04_5.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nCharacterization of protein complexes in extracellular vesicles by intact extracellular vesicle crosslinking mass spectrometry (iEVXL)\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nAbstract\nExtracellular vesicles (EVs) are blood‐borne messengers that coordinate signalling between different tissues and organs in the body. The specificity of such crosstalk is determined by preferential EV docking to target sites, as mediated through protein‐protein interactions. As such, the need to structurally characterize the EV surface precedes further understanding of docking selectivity and recipient‐cell uptake mechanisms. Here, we describe an intact extracellular vesicle crosslinking mass spectrometry (iEVXL) method that can be applied for structural characterization of protein complexes in EVs. By using a partially membrane‐permeable disuccinimidyl suberate crosslinker, proteins on the EV outer‐surface and inside EVs can be immobilized together with their interacting partners. This not only provides covalent stabilization of protein complexes before extraction from the membrane‐enclosed environment, but also generates a set of crosslinking distance restraints that can be used for structural modelling and comparative screening of changes in EV protein assemblies. Here we demonstrate iEVXL as a powerful approach to reveal high‐resolution information, about protein determinants that govern EV docking and signalling, and as a crucial aid in modelling docking interactions.\n\n[METHODS]\nMETHODS\nCell culture and EV isolation\nMDA‐MB‐231 (obtained from ATCC) and LM2 cells (provided by The Netherlands Cancer Institute, NKI) were cultured in DMEM supplemented with 10% fetal bovine serum (FBS; HyClone, USA), 10\xa0mM L‐glutamine, 50 U/ml penicillin and 50 μg/ml streptomycin (Lonza) in a humidified incubator at 37°C with 5% CO2. Cells were detached using 10\xa0mM EDTA/PBS for 5\xa0min at 37°C. Secretion media was prepared by depleting bovine derived EVs from the culture media. To do so, DMEM containing 20% FBS was centrifuged overnight at 100,000 × g at 4°C in a Sorvall T‐865 rotor (Thermo Fisher Scientific), filtered on a 0.22 μm Stericup device (Millipore, USA), diluted to 10% FBS, supplemented as previously described and kept at 4°C.\nFor EV secretion, cells were seeded on 10 plates (15\xa0cm in diameter) at 50% confluence and left to attach overnight. After attachment, cells were gently washed 3× with warm PBS, before addition of secretion media. Conditioned media containing secreted EVs was collected after 24 h and 48 h, fresh secretion media was added to replace the collected conditioned media. Cell viability was measured at the start and end of secretion using the Trypan Blue method, and it remained ∼95%. The conditioned media were spun down at 300 × g for 10\xa0min to deplete cells, transferred to clean 50\xa0ml Falcon tubes, spun down at 10,000 × g for 40\xa0min to deplete cell debris and larger vesicles, transferred to clean 50\xa0ml Falcon tubes and kept at 4°C. The two collections were pooled and EVs were immediately pelleted by ultracentrifugation at 120,000 × g, at 4°C for 2 h in a Sorvall T‐865 rotor. The pellet was resuspended by gentle pipetting in 10\xa0ml cold PBS supplemented with 50 μg/ml DNAse Ι (Sigma‐Aldrich), to decrease nucleosome contaminations on EV preparations. Purified EVs were finally pelleted again by ultracentrifugation at 120,000 × g at 4°C for 2 h. The EV‐pellet was thoroughly resuspended in 400 μl of PBS, spun down at 10,000 × g for 5\xa0min and the EV‐containing supernatant was kept. Aliquots were separated for further characterization of the EV populations.\nEV crosslinking and protein digestion\nMDA‐MB‐231 and LM2‐derived EVs were crosslinked using disuccinimidyl suberate (DSS; Thermo Fisher Scientific) with the optimal 0.5\xa0mM crosslinker concentration which was determined in an independent experiment (Figure S1). EV samples (at an average concentration of 7.5\xa0×\xa0109 p/ml, 0.5\xa0mg/ml protein in EV lysate) were crosslinked in 0.5\xa0mM DSS for 20\xa0min at RT. To promote crosslinking of lower abundant species, a second round of 0.5\xa0mM DSS was added for another 20\xa0min at RT. The crosslinking reaction was then quenched with 100\xa0mM TRIS pH 8.5 for 5\xa0min. Crosslinked EVs were aliquoted for further characterization. Crosslinked EVs were then lysed by thorough vortexing in 0.5% SDC, 8\xa0M Urea in 50\xa0mM ammonium bicarbonate, followed by 30\xa0min end‐to‐end rotation at 4°C and 15 cycles of sonication at 4°C (30\xa0s on, 30\xa0s off) in a Bioruptor (Diagenode, Belgium). Proteins were reduced with 4\u202fmM dithiothreitol (DTT) at RT for 60\u202fmin, alkylated with 16\u202fmM iodoacetamide (IAA) at RT for 30\u202fmin in the dark which was then quenched by addition of 4\u202fmM DTT. Proteins were first digested by addition of Lys‐C (at a 1:50 ratio (w/w); Wako, Japan) at 37°C for 2 h, followed by dilution to 2\xa0M Urea and further digestion with Trypsin (at a ratio 1:50 (w/w); Sigma Aldrich) at 37°C overnight. Protein digestions were stopped by acidification to 5% FA, and precipitated SDC was pelleted by centrifugation at 20,000 × g at 4°C for 30\xa0min. Supernatants were carefully collected, desalted using Sep‐Pak C18 cartridges (1cc; Waters, MA, USA), vacuum dried and stored at ‐20°C until further use.\nSCX fractionation and enrichment of crosslinked peptides\nThe desalted peptides were dissolved in 10% formic acid, 2.5% DMSO and loaded on a Luna 100A SCX column (50\xa0mm × 2\xa0mm, 5 μm, Phenomenex product number 00B‐4398‐B0) with the help of a C18 Opti‐Lynx trap column (4.6\xa0mm × 5\xa0mm, 49 μm, Optimize Technologies product number 11‐02874‐TA). Solvent A consisted of 0.05% formic acid, 20% acetonitrile in water and solvent B consisted of 0.05% formic acid, 20% acetonitrile, and 0.5\xa0M NaCl. The SCX gradient was: 0–10% B in 2\xa0min, 10–40% B in 7\xa0min, 40–80% B in 2.5\xa0min, 80–100% B in 4\xa0min, 100% B for 7\xa0min. One‐minute fractions were collected and pooled into six or seven approximately equal fractions by mean UV intensity. The pooled fractions were desalted with Oasis HLA 96‐well μElution plate (Waters, MA, USA), vacuum‐dried and stored at ‐80°C.\nLC‐MS/MS of crosslinked SCX fractions\nThe data was acquired with an Ultimate 3000 system (Thermo Fisher Scientific) coupled to an Orbitrap Exploris 480 mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Peptides were trapped (Dr. Maisch Reprosil C18, 3 μM, 2\xa0cm × 100 μM) for 2\xa0min in 5% solvent B at a flow rate of 300 nl/min, before being separated on an analytical column (Agilent Poroshell, EC‐C18, 2.7 μM, 50\xa0cm x 75 μM). Solvent B consisted of 0.1% formic acid in 80% acetonitrile while solvent A consisted of 0.1% formic acid in water. Crosslinked peptides were then separated in the analytical column at a fixed flow rate of 300 nl/min as follows: each SCX fraction was separated using an optimal 95\xa0min. linear gradient (ranging from 9–40% to 6–35% B) followed by a 3\xa0min steep increase to 99% B, a 5\xa0min wash at 99% B and a 10\xa0min re‐equilibration step at 5% A. The mass spectrometer was operated in a data‐dependent mode (DDA). Peptides were ionized in a nESI source at 1.9\xa0kV and focused at 40% amplitude of the RF lens. Full scan MS1 spectra from 350 ‐ 2200\xa0m/z were acquired in the Orbitrap at a resolution of 60,000 with the AGC target set to 3×106 and under automated calculation of maximum injection time. Cycle time for MS2 fragmentation scans was set to 2\xa0s. Only peptides with charged states 3–8 were fragmented, and dynamic exclusion was set to a duration of 16\xa0ms. Fragmentation was done using a stepped HCD collision energy strategy (NCEs: 28, 31, 34%). Fragment ions were accumulated until a target value of 1\xa0×\xa0105 ions was reached under an automated calculation of maximum injection time, with an isolation window of 1.4\xa0m/z before injection in the Orbitrap for MS2 analysis at a resolution of 30,000. The mass spectrometry proteomics data have been deposited to the ProteomeXchange Consortium via the PRIDE (Vizcaíno et\xa0al.,) partner repository with the dataset identifier PXD029591.\nCrosslinking database search and data analysis\nFor crosslinked peptide analyses, spectra were extracted from “raw” files from precursors ranging between 350 and 20,000\xa0Da, filtered by signal‐to‐noise ratio of 2 and converted to MGF format using Proteome Discovered software (v2.4, Thermo Scientific). MGF files were searched in pLink 2 (Chen et\xa0al.,) against a database containing the 1000 most abundant proteins of both MDA‐MB‐231 and LM2 EV proteomes, as determined in section\xa04.10., and appended with the sequences of common FBS contaminants to avoid misidentification of crosslinked peptide sequences from Bos taurus. The database was further curated to remove signal peptides from the protein sequences (note that positions within proteins still follow the Uniprot numbering). DSS was set as non‐cleavable crosslinker and trypsin was set as the digestion enzyme, and up to three missed cleavages were allowed. Peptide length was set to between six and 60 amino‐acids, precursor and fragment ion tolerance were set to 10 and 20\xa0ppm, respectively, and oxidation of methionine and acetylation of protein N‐terminus were set as variable modifications while carbamidomethylation of cysteines was set as a fixed modification. FDR was set at 1% at all levels, which was calculated by using a reverse decoy database strategy. E‐scores were not computed to minimize processing times.\nCrosslinking and proteomics data were analysed using Excel and in‐house built R scripts (R Development Core Team, R), and plots refined with Illustrator 2020 (Adobe, USA). Spectra and site files from pLink were processed to generate the tables in the supplementary data, and the scripts used to curate pLink outputs have been deposited in GitHub (https://github.com/hecklab/pLink‐results‐analysis). Only crosslinks identified with sufficient spectral evidence (≥ 2 CSM per cell line) were kept while crosslinks involving histones, a debatable contaminant in EV preparations, were not analysed further. In cases of protein ID ambiguity due to shared crosslinked pept\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
